{"id": "sevt_01NfAAznPPi1V9FPuibHkYV7", "content": [{"text": "**Checkout p99 moved from about 78 ms to 2,201 ms at 14:38 and stayed at 4 seconds or more through 14:59.** The checkout deploy `de6541f` explains it.\n\n**Deploy timeline vs. metrics**\n- `de6541f` (checkout, alice, 14:38:10) \u2192 checkout p99 jumps at 14:38 (2,201 ms, then 3,739 ms at 14:39).\n- `a9efec4` (inventory, carol, 14:39:40) \u2192 inventory p99 rises at 14:39 (244 ms, then about 400 ms+). This is after checkout moved, so it doesn't explain the onset. It may add load, but I haven't verified that.\n- Checkout error rate stays at 0% until 14:46, then runs at roughly 0.1\u20130.75% and reaches about 0.4\u20130.5% by 14:59.\n\n**Diff `de6541f`**\nThe commit is labeled a readability refactor, but it changes `build_summary` from one batched query (`OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)`, grouped in memory) to one `OrderItem` query per open order inside a loop. This is the \"per-row query where there used to be a batch\" pattern from the runbook.\n\n**Log confirmation**\n- `order_items` queries were batched (`IN (?)`) at 3\u20137 per minute before the deploy. From 14:38 on they are per-row, at roughly 80\u2013180 per minute.\n- From 14:59 the checkout hosts log `DB connection pool exhausted (active=50/50, waiters=12)` and `PoolTimeout` 500s, and `db_pool` utilization is at 43\u201344/50. Pool exhaustion is the downstream effect of the extra queries. The 500s show up in the error rate from 14:46.\n\n**Fix:** revert `de6541f` (or restore the batched query) and redeploy checkout. Inventory's cache change should be reviewed separately, since INC-2241 is still open for it.\n\nRoot cause: `de6541f` \u2014 the refactor replaced a single batched `order_items` query in `build_summary` with one query per open order, so each checkout request now runs N extra DB round-trips, which pushes p99 to multiple seconds and exhausts the DB connection pool.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:47:09.958669Z", "type": "agent.message"}
